Metal-organic-frameworks (MOFs), made by linking inorganic and organic units with strong bonds (reticular synthesis)[1l have emerged as promising platforms for diverse applications, including gas storage,[2] catalysis,[3] and separation.[4] Owing to their finely tunable chemical composition, morphology and robust structure, a very recent advanced application in MOFs chemistry was used as a protective networks for immobilizing functional biomacromolecules, e.g. DNA, proteins and enzymes in the fields of nanomachine,[5]  biosensing[6]  and  biocatalysis.[7]  This immobilization offers effective control of the reaction processes and enhanced biomacromolecules stability in storage and operational conditions.[8]  However, biomacromolecules are susceptible to degradation and denaturation, and thus current immobilization strategies are usually carried out through the impregnation of the biomacromolecules into the pre-synthesized materials with large pore dimensions.[5-7,9] It faces substantial challenges, including low loading efficiency, incompact confinement and the restriction of the pore size.
Encasing a protein or enzymes within a MOFs that possessed significantly smaller pore dimensions than the biomacromolecule was recently achieved via a mild de novo approach, such as biomimetic mineralization.[10] Unfortunately, the surface charge and chemistry of a protein or enzyme strongly determined its ability to be encapsulated into the MOFs, where only those with low surface electrostatic potential were capable of concentrating the metal cations and inducing the encapsulation process.[11] Moreover, there are cases in which it takes a long times (several days) to engender MOFs formation. Thus, a high-efficiency and general approach for engendering biomacromoleculesencapsulated MOFs is highly desirable yet not reported.
Herein,we presented a new amino acid-boosted one pot embedding(AAOPE）strategy that enabled torapidly encapsulate/coencapsulate a broad range of proteins or enzymes within MOFs with a high loading efficiency. The key principle relied on the accelerating prenucleated clusters around proteins, through the form of protenis/polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP)/cysteine (Cys) self-assembly. This inspiration stemmed from the metal cations-accumulating pattern of the metallothioneins[12] (Figure 1A), wherein the proteins assembled with abundant Cys favored the accumulation of $Z n^{2+}$ ions by mercaptide bonds,and thus accelerated prenucleated clusters and engendered MOFs around the proteins (Figure 1B). As a proof for our strategy, we firstly chose a low-cytotoxicity MOFs material， zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZlF-8), formed by coordination between $Z n^{2+}$ and 2- methylimidazole (mlM).[13] The twelve proteins, enzymes and even other cargos (e.g. nanoparticles) with different surface chemistries were successfully encapsulated into ZlF-8 within several minutes. In addition, this versatile encapsulation strategy was also feasible for other MOFs,HKUST-1. Remarkably,the high-encapsulating proteins could maintain their native structural conformations, and the tightness of structural confinement within ZIF-8 endowed the enzymes with excellent bioactivity even in harsh conditions that specifically denature or inhibit free enzymes. Furthermore, the feasible of the AAOPE strategy for biostorage, catalytic cascades and biosensing were also explored. To our knowledge, it is the first time that a general protein surface charge-independent strategy for engendering protein- or enzymes-encapsulated MOFs is introduced.
Metallothioneins, a group of Cys rich proteins (Figure 1A), are able to accumulate metal cations via the coordination interaction between Cys and metal cations.[12] We hypothesized that the decoration of Cys may accelerated prenucleated clusters with ligands and metal ions around the proteins, and triggered the formation of proteins-encapsulated MOFs. Even so, the weak interaction between proteins and amino acid in water solution stil remains a barrier. Another key hypothesis to our strategy is that
