the PVP， a biocompatible macromolecule analogous to proteins,[14] can be adapted to enrich the Cys on proteins, because: 1) PVP is a capping agent that is capable of forming a PVP/proteins complex with the proteins;[14] and 2) the abundant inherent amide groups may favor guiding the amino acids towards protein through hydrogen bond. Motivated by the hypothesis, we firstly started the synthesis of myoglobin (MB)-inducted MOFs, because MB has a positive surface charge (isoelectric point \~ 8.7, Table S1) that excludes the $Z n^{2+}$ and restrains ZiF-8 formation. As anticipated, the system instantaneously turned from transparent to opaque within 3 min using our AAOPE strategy (Supporting Movie S1), and a brown precipitate was observed after 1 h (Figure 1C, below, and Figure S1).As a comparison, the control system without Cys still kept transparent even after $12\mathsf{h}$ ， and no precipitate was formed (Figure 1C, above). The powder Xray diffraction (PXRD) measurements demonstrated that the MBinduced precipitates have a standard Bragg diffraction pattern of ZIF-8 (Figure 1D).
We further investigated the effect of the proteins dosage on the formation of ZlF-8. ZIF-8 precipitates were observed along with the dosage of proteins increased from 0.5 mg to $2\mathsf{m g}$ ，whileless or no precipitates were formed when further enhancing the dosage of proteins (Figure S2). PXRD and scanning electron microscope (SEM)were also used to explore the evolvement of crystallinity and morphology, respectively. As the dosage of MB improved from 0.5 mg to $2.0\ \mathsf{m g},$ the Bragg diffraction peaks showed dramatically diminished clarity (Figure 1D), indicating the decreased framework crystallinity. Meanwhile, the ZlF-8 crystals trend to grow into a small sphere with nanometer scale $(\sim50\:\mathsf{n m})$ _， as confirmed by the SEM image (Figure 1E). It meant that we could control the size and morphology of the proteins-inducted ZIF-8 through adjusting the dosage of the proteins. Moreover, a closer interrogation revealed that the dosage of Cys also had an analogous effect on the formation of ZlF-8 (Figure S3). These results indicated that both the proteins and Cys have an impact on the protein-seeded clusters and thus influencing the formation of ZIF-8 around the proteins.
Given that the MB-inducted ZlF-8 was confirmed, we next ascertained that the MB-encapsulated-ZIF-8 (MB@ZIF-8)was indeed produced.A phenomenon that raised our interest was that the supernatant became colourless after 1 h reaction (Figure 1C and Figure S4), indicating that all of the MB might be successfully encapsulated into ZIF-8. The encapsulation of MB was firstly confirmed by a control experiment, which was implemented by mixing equivalent pre-synthesized ZIF-8 crystals and MB. It revealed that only $20.14\%$ of the MB could be adsorbed onto the surface of ZiF-8 crystals (Figure S5). PVP surface adsorbant exchange and sodium dodecylsulfate (SDS) washing treatment resulted in release of MB into the solution for the MB-adsorbed, but not the MB-encapsulated ZIF-8,[15] thereby further confirming MB-encapsulated-ZIF-8 was indeed synthesized. The PVP exchange experiment gave an estimate of the encapsulated rate as high as $\sim97\ \%$ (estimated as $\sim18.2$ wt $\%$ loading),which was in agreement with the ones obtained by SDS washing experiment $(\sim94.5\%$ encapsulated rate, equated to be ${\sim}17.7\$ wt $\%$ loading, Figure S6). Remarkably, this encapsulation efficiency was higher than most of the reported protein-embedded MOFs using de novo and Infiltration approach,[14a,16] and proteins-loaded nanoparticles through adsorption[17] (Table S2). Additional evidence of the encapsulation of proteins was supported by the formation of ZIF8 sample using fluorescein isothiocyanate labelled MB (FiTC-MB) The FITC-MB-inducted ZIF-8 crystals was exposed to ultraviolet light after SDS washing, whereon a green fluorescence with the emission at characteristic of FITC $(525\mathsf{n m})$ )was observed.(Figure 2A). Furthermore, confocal microscopy revealed that the MB homogenously distributed within ZiF-8 (the inset in Figure 2A).
We confirmed the self-assembly among proteins, PVP and Cys by Uv-vis spectrum (Figure S12), and demonstrated that the mechanism of the formation of proteins-encapsulated-ZiF-8 was attributed to the accelerated prenucleated clusters around the proteins, owing to the form of protenis/PVP)/Cys self-assembly (details seen in supporting information section 2 and Figure S11- S19). The amino acid-boosted encapsulation offered the capacity of preparing protein- or enzyme-encapsulated-ZlF-8 that could ignore their surface chemical properties. The other five proteins with positive surface charge (Table S1), such as HRP, cytochrome c (Cyt C), ribonuclease A (RNase A), ribonuclease B (RNase B) and trypsin were also able to induce the formation of proteins@ZIF-8 using the AAOPE strategy,while none of them were capable of driving the formation of ZiF-8 using the reported biomineralized method (Figure S20)[10] The SEM, PXRD,FTIR and SDS washing experiments well confirmed that the proteins were encapsulated into the framework (Figure S21-S23). In addition, the general encapsulation was further demonstrated by using other four kinds of proteins (Figure S21B and Figure S24). Furthermore, this biomimetic strategy was also applicable for encapsulating other cargos (e.g. gold nanoparticle magnetic nanoparticle and quantum dots, Figure S25- S27), and feasible for extending to other MOFs (e.g. HKUST-1, Figure S28 and S29) We next explored whether the formed ZiF-8 protective layer could allow the enzymes with high stability under denaturing conditions. We firstly exposed the HRP-encapsulated ZIF-8 (HRP@ZIF-8, ${\sim}14.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ loading,Figure S30) and free HRP to the trypsin,which is a proteolytic agent that could devitalize the enzymes through hydrolysis. The bioactivity of HRP $@$ ZIF-8 maintained $86.7\%$ while that of the free HRP decreased to $22.4\%$ (Figure 3A), indicating the micropore network of ZiF-8 was capable of excluding the trypsin through size-selective channel, and thus prevented the HRP from hydrolysis.
To further assess whether such high bioactivity conversion was attributed to the tight structural confinement within the MOFs provided by the AAOPE approach, we prepared another enzyme composite, in which HRP was loosely confined in the ${\mathsf{C a C O}}_{3}$ particles with 30-50 nm mesopores, denoted as ${\mathsf{H R P@C a C O_{3}}}$ (Figure S37). As shown in Figure 3A, the corresponding activity conversions of ${\mathsf{H R P}}@{\mathsf{C a C O}}_{3}$ were much lower compared to those of HRP $@$ ZIF-8. These comparative tests provided another evidence that the tightness of structural confinement endowed the enzyme with excellent stability.
The feasibility of the AAOPE strategy for biotechnological and biocatalytic application was then explored. We firstly constructed a multi-purposenanochamberforbiospecimen(e.g. glycoproteins) concentration, storage and analysis. The concept of such AAOPE-based nanochamber was presented in Figure S38A. Briefly, Target glycoproteins were feasible to be spontaneously concentrated $(>96\%$ encapsulating efficiency) and stored under room temperature (Figure S38B and S38C), and the pH-trigger collapse of the ZlF-8 framework allowed the release of encapsulated glycoproteins in an extreme sample manner (Figure S39). The real-world application of this nanochamber was demonstrated by different proteins storage and analysis in human serum (Figure S40).
The enzyme cascade is defined as a chain of chemical reactions proceeding in a concurrent fashion, having gained much attention.[20] The versatile AAOPE strategy encouraged us to prepare multi-enzymes-coencapsulated ZiF-8 that act as enzyme cascade nanoreactors[21] (ECN, Figure 4A, Figure S41A and S42) The coencapsulation of two enzymes (HRP and GOx) was confirmed through the repeated experiment using FiTC-labelled GOx and Rhodamine B-labelled HRP (Figure S43 and S44). Furthermore, confocal microscopy further revealed that both of the HRP and $\mathsf{G O x}$ homogenously arranged within ZiF-8 (Figure 4B),which favored for biocatalytic cascades.As expected,the ECN leaded to ${\sim}3$ fold enhancements in the activity of the catalytic cascades, compared with the bulk mixture of the free enzymes in solution (Figure 4C). Such accelerating effect caused by the yielded high local concentrations of the substrates at the intercommunicated enzymes in ECN. In addition, this ECN was also successfully applied for sensing glucose with naked eyes (Figure 4D and Figure S45), and the sensitivity was superior to the ECN fabricated by co-precipitation approach,[21a] and also superior to most of the reported colorimetric glucose sensors (Table S3). Remarkably, the sensing performance of ECN showed a high conversion even keeping it at room temperature for 15 d, and exposed it to $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (1 h)or DMF (1 h) (Figure 4E). Such stable performance was also comparable with other reported ECN.[21] These findings have shown the high promise of our AAOPE strategy for catalytic cascades and biosensing.
In summary, we present a versatile biomimetic strategy for high-efficientencapsulationofbiomacromoleculesor nanoparticles within MOFs. This strategy, relies on the Cystriggered prenucleated clusters around proteins, is general applicable to encapsulate a broad range of proteins and enzymes in a high-loading manner. In addition, the encapsulated proteins well maintain their native conformations, and the tightness of structural confinement within assembled MOFs endows the enzyme with excellent stability. The encapsulation is protein surface charge-independent and nondestructive, the speed is remarkable, and the controlled release of the encapsulated proteins can be achieved via simple pH modification. These features endow it with huge potential not only in chemical processing, but also in biotechnology.
